    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite a LinkedIn post of up to 500 words about a lesson learned from shipping a feature that nobody used.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n(One of the texts is a real LinkedIn post by a person, written on its own topic; judge tells, not topic.)\n\n=== TEXT A ===\n7 Chrome extensions that boost productivity so much they feel illegal to use.\n\nAll free:\n\n1. Bionic Reading®  - Guides your eyes by highlighting the initial letters of each word. A fantastic way to read longer texts (e.g., blog posts). I use it primarily with Medium.\n\n2. StayFocusd - Blocks time-wasting websites. I have repeatedly found that eliminating distractions is essential to focus on deep work (a flow state).\n\n3. Glasp  - Allows you to take notes and add highlights on web pages. Everyone should know it. It delivers what it promises.\n\n4. ScribeHow - Allows you to record the screen and immediately convert any procedure (e.g., a sequence of steps in the app) into a tutorial. A great way to share knowledge in the organization.\n\n5. Calendly - Eliminate the hassle of back-and-forth emails to find the perfect meeting time—an excellent, free alternative to Microsoft Bookings.\n\n6. Grammarly - Correct spelling, grammar, punctuation, clarity, etc., as you write. You can use the free version, although it is worth paying for. After a few months, I can't imagine working without it.\n\n7. Todoist - A great way to organize & monitor tasks and plan your days. The feature I love is adding sites as tasks, e.g., LinkedIn posts, tweets, Substack articles, etc. It definitely speeds up my learning.\n\nI do not use: \n- AI writing assistants\n- AI paraphrases\n- Content schedulers\n\nSo I have not included any of them.\n\nDid I miss something important?\n\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nTwo years ago I spent about six weeks building a feature that, as far as I can tell, was used by eleven people. I am not rounding.\n\nIt was a scheduling tool inside a reporting product. Customers could set up a report to generate itself every Monday and land in their inbox. We had heard the request in at least a dozen customer calls. I had a spreadsheet of quotes. When we shipped it, the announcement email got a decent open rate and a few replies saying \"finally.\" Then nothing. Usage flatlined within a month and never recovered.\n\nFor a while I told myself the problem was discoverability. We moved the button. We added a tooltip. We mentioned it in onboarding. Still nothing. Eventually I did the thing I should have done first, which was call the people who had asked for it and ask why they weren't using it.\n\nThe answer was boring and kind of humbling. Most of them already had a workaround. They had a calendar reminder, or an assistant who pulled the report, or a habit of checking on Monday mornings with their coffee. The workaround was slightly annoying, which is why they mentioned it when we asked what was annoying. But it was not annoying enough to change a routine they had already built. The request was real. The pain was real. The pain just wasn't big enough to justify learning something new, even something small.\n\nI had been treating a complaint as a commitment. Those are very different things. People will happily tell you what bothers them. They are much less likely to tell you, or even know, what they would actually change their behavior for. And I had never asked the follow up question that matters, which is some version of \"what are you doing about this today?\" If the answer is \"nothing, I just live with it,\" that's a signal the pain is tolerable, and tolerable pain rarely gets a feature adopted.\n\nThe other thing I learned is that the sunk cost after shipping is worse than the sunk cost before. Once the feature existed, we kept spending small amounts of time on it. Fixing an edge case here, answering a support ticket there, making sure a redesign didn't break it. It sat in the codebase like a houseguest nobody invited but nobody wanted to be rude to. We finally removed it last spring. Nobody wrote in.\n\nI still ask customers what frustrates them. I just don't stop there anymore. I ask what they've tried, how often it actually comes up, and what they'd give up to have it fixed. Most of the time the honest answer is \"not much,\" and I've learned to take that as useful information rather than a challenge.\n\nThe feature didn't fail because we built it badly. It failed because we confused being asked for something with being needed. That distinction has probably saved me more time than anything I've shipped since.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
